rules_go translates the JRE's os.arch into a GOARCH, but the table only
covers aarch64 and x86_64, so on a 32-bit x86 host "i386" passes through
unchanged and the generated toolchains reference @rules_go//go/toolchain:i386,
which does not exist -- the GOARCH is spelled "386", as go/private/platforms.bzl
already has it.

The i686 -> 386 case used to be there; it was dropped in cf20167d (#3282),
which replaced the uname-based detection with ctx.os.arch and kept only two
of the translations.

The print() is here to record which spelling this box actually reports, since
os.arch may also be i686 or x86.  CI only.

--- a/go/private/sdk.bzl
+++ b/go/private/sdk.bzl
@@ -470,10 +470,13 @@ def detect_host_platform(ctx):
         goos = "windows"
 
     goarch = ctx.os.arch
+    print("rules_go-386.patch: ctx.os.arch = " + goarch)
     if goarch == "aarch64":
         goarch = "arm64"
     elif goarch == "x86_64":
         goarch = "amd64"
+    elif goarch in ("i386", "i486", "i586", "i686", "i786", "x86"):
+        goarch = "386"
 
     return goos, goarch
